An alternative way due to Marsaglia [26.53] is simple, fast, and seems to be well suited to high-speed computations. Let \(p\)1 for _i_=1, 2, . . . , \(n\) be expressed by \(k\) decimal digits as \(p_{t}\)=\(\delta_{tt}\).\(\delta_{tt}\) . . . \(\delta_{tt}\) where the \(\delta\)'s are the decimal digits. (If the domain of the random variable is infinite, it is necessary to truncate the probability distribution at \(p_{n}\).) Define

\(P_{0}\)=0, \(P_{r}\)=10\({}^{-r}\sum\limits_{l=1}^{n}\delta_{tt}\) for _r_=1, 2, . . . , \(k\), and

\(\Pi_{l}\)=\(\sum\limits_{r=1}^{t}10^{r}P_{r}\), _s_=1, 2, . . . . , \(k\).

Number the computer memory locations by 0, 1, 2, . . . , \(\Pi_{k}\)=1. The memory locations are divided into \(k\) mutually exclusive sets such that the _s_th set consists of memory locations \(\Pi_{t-1}\), \(\Pi_{t-1}\)+1, . . . , \(\Pi_{t}\)=1. The information stored in the memory locations of the _s_th set consists of \(y_{1}\) in \(\delta_{tt}\) locations, \(y_{2}\) in \(\delta_{tt}\) locations, . . . , \(y_{n}\) in \(\delta_{tt}\) locations.

